                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_barrel:        `<strong>That’s the governance read.</strong> The controls didn’t fail at random — the individual league table, the target pressure and the fear of speaking up <em>manufactured</em> the behaviour. Name it as a bad barrel and you can fix the system; call it bad apples and you’ll be back here next quarter with a new set of names.`,
                    coach_r1_controls:      `<strong>“The checks just failed” is half the diagnosis.</strong> Tightening approvals does stop today’s bleeding — but the checks failed <em>because</em> the incentive culture pushed people to work around them. A control gap on top of a toxic reward system is a symptom, not the cause.`,
                    coach_r1_badapples:     `<strong>“A few bad apples” is exactly the framing the data refuses.</strong> 41% of your sellers report target pressure and the flagged branches are the top of a league table you designed. Suspend seven people and the next cohort does the same thing next quarter — the barrel is still rotten. What in the <em>system</em> produced this?`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You linked the misconduct to the league table, read the 8% speak-up rate as suppressed signal rather than reassurance, and treated the small numbers as an early signature, not a contained event. That’s the bad-barrel read — and it’s the only one that lets you fix the cause.`,
                    coach_r1_numbers:       `<strong>The small numbers are the trap.</strong> “Only 18 branches, only 3 whistleblowing reports in two years” is how a systemic problem stays invisible. A low report count with 41% pressure isn’t reassurance — it’s suppressed signal. The numbers are the early signature, not the ceiling.`,
                    coach_r1_speakup:       `<strong>Reading 8% speak-up as reassuring is the classic misread.</strong> A low whistleblowing rate where 41% feel pressure is not the absence of a problem — it’s fear suppressing the signal. Treat silence as a red flag, not an all-clear.`,
                    coach_r1_zero:          `<strong>Zero un-flagged branches at risk?</strong> The 18 flagged branches run the same league table as the other 192. If the barrel is the cause, the risk lives across the network — estimating zero is the bad-apples story in a spreadsheet cell.`,
                    coach_r2_both:          `<strong>That’s the sequenced answer.</strong> Immediate controls stop today’s bleeding while real culture work — incentive redesign, psychological safety, accountability — addresses the cause. Controls buy you time; culture spends it well. This is the mix that lowers the misconduct-risk index for good.`,
                    coach_r2_bothlight:     `<strong>Right instinct, thin on culture.</strong> You’ve named both levers, but the culture effort is light — and it’s the culture lever that actually moves the structural risk. Controls alone plateau; lean harder into incentives, speak-up and tone before the index stalls.`,
                    coach_r2_controlsonly:  `<strong>Controls only is the rules-only trap.</strong> Second-signatures and monitoring stop today’s bleeding — and Satkunasingam’s research is blunt: layered on a toxic incentive culture, they push misconduct underground, not away. Your misconduct-risk index will not fall below the 70s while the league table that caused it stands.`,
                    coach_r2_cultureonly:   `<strong>Culture without controls leaves the bleeding unstopped.</strong> Tone and incentives are the cure, but you have live mis-selling at 18 branches today. Trust-and-fix-the-culture is the right destination; you still need consent checks and monitoring to stop the harm while it lands.`,
                    coach_r2_rulesonly_trap:`<strong>Heavy controls, almost no culture — the textbook rules-only trap.</strong> You’re doubling monitoring while the individual league table and the fear of speaking up stay exactly as they were. The index won’t move, and the behaviour resurfaces in the branches audit hasn’t looked at yet.`,
                    coach_r3_redesign:      `<strong>Redesigning the incentive is the governance move.</strong> Shifting from individual league tables to balanced, customer-outcome metrics fixes the cause without abolishing growth — governance isn’t anti-growth, it’s growth that lasts. The misconduct-risk index falls because the thing that produced the behaviour is gone.`,
                    coach_r3_scrap:         `<strong>You killed the league table overnight — conduct risk drops, but so does growth.</strong> Fee-income growth falls ~9 points and your best performers feel punished for the bank’s design failure. Governance isn’t anti-growth; could you redesign incentives toward customer outcomes rather than just abolish them?`,
                    coach_r3_keep:          `<strong>Keeping the league table guarantees recurrence.</strong> You’ve accepted the “healthy competition” story — but 41% pressure, an 8% speak-up rate and 2,400 possibly mis-sold customers aren’t healthy competition; they’re the dashboard of a conduct failure. Leave the incentive intact and no control framework compensates.`,
                    coach_r3_selfreport:    `<strong>Self-reporting now is the disclosure that protects the bank.</strong> BNM rewards prompt candour and punishes concealment severely. A self-reported RM 14M remediation stays a remediation; discovered late, it becomes a RM 30M enforcement action and a mandated independent review. You’ve bought supervisory trust.`,
                    coach_r3_investigate:   `<strong>“Investigate fully before troubling the regulator” is the delay trap.</strong> While your review drags on, BNM’s thematic review can land and find it first — and late discovery turns self-disclosure into enforcement. What does the delay actually protect: the bank, or the people who’d rather it stayed quiet?`,
                    coach_r3_hold:          `<strong>Holding disclosure is the catastrophic choice.</strong> BNM expects prompt self-reporting; conceal it and when the regulator finds the pattern independently — and it will — the RM 14M remediation becomes a RM 30M fine, a mandated review, and a supervisory relationship in ruins. Concealment doesn’t protect the bank; it protects the silence.`,
                    coach_r3_frontline:     `<strong>Disciplining only the frontline is bad-apples in action.</strong> The seven sellers were the top of a league table you designed. Hold them accountable and leave the managers and designers who built the pressure untouched, and you’ve signalled the system is fine — which guarantees the next cohort does the same thing.`,
                    coach_r3_norem:         `<strong>Zero remediation reads as denial.</strong> The ~2,400 customers were sold insurance they didn’t want; refusing to make them whole until forced is the opposite of the candour BNM and the board are watching for. Provisioning the remediation is both the ethical and the cheaper-than-enforcement choice.`,
                    coach_r4_getahead:      `<strong>Getting ahead of it is the only defensible move.</strong> Briefing BNM and the board chair before the whistleblower’s email and the thematic review do it for you keeps you the narrator of your own conduct failure. Transparency builds supervisory trust; discovery destroys it — you’ve chosen the former.`,
                    coach_r4_delay:         `<strong>Postponing the board is delay dressed as diligence.</strong> The whistleblower has already emailed the chair; the review is already coming. Buying two more weeks of silence only widens the gap between what the board knows and what the regulator is about to find.`,
                    coach_r4_deny:          `<strong>Deny-and-contain is how a remediation becomes a scandal.</strong> Telling the journalist there’s no story while BNM’s review is live is a bet you cannot win — when it lands, the cover-up is the headline, not the mis-selling. You’ve converted a manageable conduct issue into an enforcement event.`,
                    coach_r4_rootcause:     `<strong>That’s the honest one-sentence diagnosis.</strong> Naming the incentive culture as the root cause — and committing to fix the system, not just the people — is exactly what keeps the board from being made complicit. The chair can defend that to the regulator; “isolated lapses” would have unravelled the moment the whistleblower’s email surfaced.`,
                    coach_r4_isolated:      `<strong>“Isolated control lapses, now remediated” buries the root cause.</strong> The board chair has just received a whistleblower email describing target pressure across the network. If your brief doesn’t name the incentive culture, you’ve made the board complicit in the next scandal. Rewrite the opening: what is the honest diagnosis?`,
                    coach_r4_competition:   `<strong>You let the “healthy competition” framing win.</strong> 41% pressure, an 8% speak-up rate and 2,400 possibly mis-sold customers aren’t healthy competition — they’re a conduct failure. Whose interest does that story serve, and what evidence would change that person’s mind?`,
                    coach_r4_sales:         `<strong>Committing to measure sales volume is committing to the cause.</strong> The cross-sell ratios are exactly what built the pressure. If the only dashboard the board sees is volume, you’ve told the organisation that conduct still comes second — and rebuilt the conditions for the next mis-selling wave.`,

