                    insight_recovery:       `Culture-led recovery: you diagnosed the bad barrel, sequenced controls with real culture work, redesigned the incentive toward customer outcomes, self-reported to BNM and held the system — not just the frontline — accountable. The misconduct-risk index fell for good, the regulator’s trust held, and the board backed a brief that named the root cause honestly.`,
                    insight_concealment:    `Concealment catastrophe: by holding or denying disclosure, you let BNM discover the pattern independently. The self-reportable RM 14M remediation became a RM 30M enforcement action with a mandated independent review, and supervisory trust — the thing that takes years to build — collapsed. Discovery destroys what disclosure protects.`,
                    insight_rulesonly:      `Rules-only trap: you tightened controls but left the individual league table that caused the misconduct intact. Exactly as Satkunasingam warns, controls on a toxic incentive culture pushed the behaviour underground — the misconduct-risk index stayed in the 70s and the pattern is set to resurface in the branches audit hasn’t looked at yet.`,
                    insight_badapples:      `Bad-apples trap: you disciplined the frontline and closed the file while the incentive system that produced the behaviour stayed untouched. Seven names changed; the barrel didn’t. With 41% of sellers still under pressure, the next cohort is already on the same path.`,
                    insight_overcorrect:    `Over-correction: you abolished the incentive scheme wholesale instead of redesigning it. Conduct risk dropped, but fee-income growth fell ~9 points and your best performers were punished for the bank’s design failure. The governance goal was better incentives, not no incentives.`,
                    insight_sanitized:      `Your board brief buried the cultural root cause behind “isolated lapses” or “healthy competition.” With a whistleblower already emailing the chair, a sanitised brief makes the board complicit in the next scandal — and the candour the regulator rewards was the one thing it lacked.`,
